## Vitamin D — Evidence Summary

The NHS states that vitamin D "helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body," and that these nutrients "are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust notes that vitamin D "exerts an affect primarily on calcium metabolism," with calcitriol upregulating absorption of calcium and phosphate from the gut and reabsorption of calcium in the renal tubules, and that "vitamin D receptors have been found on many different cell and tissues" (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/vitamin-d). East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust additionally states that vitamin D "has a role in immune function and cell division" and "has effects on many different organs and tissues all over the body" (https://www.esht.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/0926.pdf).

Regarding food sources, the NHS lists oily fish (such as salmon, sardines and mackerel), egg yolks, and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals (https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/bone-health/food-for-strong-bones). NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly adds that egg yolks, meat and milk contain a small amount of vitamin D, and that most vitamin D comes from the action of UV rays on the skin when outdoors (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d).

On the question of how much vitamin D adults need each day in the UK: this is a sourced factual matter and the available evidence is set out here. NICE states that "currently there is no reference nutrient intake for people aged between 4 and 65," while confirming a reference nutrient intake of 10 micrograms per day for adults aged 65 and over, and during pregnancy and lactation (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/resources/implementing-vitamin-d-guidance-draft-guideline2). NICE also records that "the reference nutrient intake for population groups is 10 micrograms/day (1 microgram = 40 international units, so 10 micrograms = 400 IU)" (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/resources/vitamin-d-supplement-use-in-specific-population-groups-pdf-1996421765317). NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly states that Public Health England recommends everyone over the age of one year considers taking a supplement of 10 micrograms (400 IU) per day during autumn and winter months (October to March) (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d). These are the figures recorded by those sources; I record and cite sources rather than advise on personal intake.

Buckinghamshire NHS Formulary records that both EFSA and the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) concluded that an upper limit of 4,000 IU (100 micrograms) per day is safe for adults and children over 11 years of age, including pregnant and lactating women (https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf).

