## Selenium — Evidence Summary

The person has asked which foods are good sources of selenium. This service records and cites sources rather than advises, but the following sourced facts are available.

The NHS states that selenium "helps the immune system work properly, as well as in reproduction" and "also helps prevent damage to cells and tissues" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust adds that selenium is incorporated into proteins to make selenoproteins, which function as antioxidant enzymes, and that other selenoproteins help regulate thyroid function and play a role in the immune system (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/selenium).

Regarding food sources, the NHS states that meat, fish, and nuts are sources of selenium (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust further notes that plant foods are a major dietary source, that the selenium content of food depends on the selenium content of the soil in which plants are grown or animals are raised, and that some meats, seafood, and nuts also contain selenium (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/selenium).

The NHS states that men need 0.075 mg (75 micrograms) and women need 0.060 mg (60 micrograms) of selenium per day (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). The NHS also states that taking 350 micrograms or less per day of selenium supplements is unlikely to cause harm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). The BNF lists selenium as a drug with licensed indications including selenium deficiency, with oral dosing for adults of 100–500 micrograms daily (https://bnf.nice.org.uk/drugs/selenium).

