## Selenium — Evidence Summary

The person's question asks which foods are good sources of selenium. I record and cite sources rather than advise; the relevant sourced facts are set out below.

The NHS states that selenium "helps the immune system work properly, as well as in reproduction" and "also helps prevent damage to cells and tissues" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). South Tees NHS Trust notes that selenium is incorporated into proteins to make selenoproteins, which function as antioxidant enzymes, and that other selenoproteins help regulate thyroid function and play a role in the immune system (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/selenium).

Regarding food sources, the NHS lists brazil nuts, fish, meat, and eggs as good sources of selenium (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust additionally lists meat (especially chicken), fish, brown rice, wholegrain cereals, wholemeal bread, brazil and cashew nuts, vegetables such as spinach and mushrooms, baked beans, eggs, and liver and liver pâté as among the best food sources (https://www.leedsth.nhs.uk/patients/resources/selenium). South Tees NHS Trust states that plant foods are the major dietary sources of selenium, and that it can also be found in some meats, seafood, and nuts (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/selenium).

The NHS states the reference intake for selenium is 75 µg per day for men aged 19 to 64, and 60 µg per day for women aged 19 to 64 (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). The NHS also states that taking 350 µg or less per day of selenium supplements is unlikely to cause harm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others).

