**Selenium — Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that selenium helps the immune system work properly, supports reproduction, and helps prevent damage to cells and tissues (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust additionally notes that selenium is incorporated into proteins to make selenoproteins, which function as antioxidant enzymes, and that other selenoproteins help regulate thyroid function and play a role in the immune system (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/selenium). Newcastle upon Tyne Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust describes selenium as an essential trace element and a cofactor for numerous enzymes, including the glutathione peroxidase family (https://laboratories.newcastle-hospitals.nhs.uk/test-directory/selenium-serum).

Regarding food sources — which is the question asked — the NHS lists brazil nuts, fish, meat, and eggs as good sources, and states that people who eat meat, fish, or nuts should be able to obtain all the selenium they need from their daily diet (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust provides a broader list of best food sources, including meat (especially chicken), fish, brown rice, wholegrain cereals (such as Cheerios, Shreddies, Shredded Wheat, and Weetabix), wholemeal bread, brazil and cashew nuts, vegetables (such as spinach and mushrooms), baked beans, eggs, and liver and liver pâté (https://www.leedsth.nhs.uk/patients/resources/selenium). South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust notes that plant foods are the major dietary sources of selenium, and that the selenium content of food depends on the selenium content of the soil in which plants are grown or animals are raised; meats, seafood, and some nuts are also noted as sources (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/selenium).

The NHS states that the UK reference intake for selenium is 75 µg per day for men aged 19 to 64 and 60 µg per day for women aged 19 to 64 (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). The NHS further states that taking 350 µg or less per day of selenium supplements is unlikely to cause harm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others).

