**Vitamin D — Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, and that these nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth, and muscles healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust additionally states that vitamin D has a role in immune function and cell division, and that it has effects on many different organs and tissues across the body (https://www.esht.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/0926.pdf).

Food sources cited by NHS sources include oily fish (such as salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring, and trout), egg yolks, meat, liver, tofu, mushrooms, and milk, with smaller amounts in canned tuna; margarine, some breakfast cereals, soya products, orange juice, and infant formula in the UK may have vitamin D added (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d; https://swlimo.southwestlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/SWL-Vitamin-D-PIL-Accessible-V1.1.pdf).

NHS sources state that the UK recommended supplementation level for adults is 10 micrograms (400 IU) per day, specifically referenced to the autumn and winter months by some sources (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d; https://swlimo.southwestlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/SWL-Vitamin-D-Guidelines-for-Adults-and-Children.pdf). The Buckinghamshire NHS Formulary records that both EFSA and the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) concluded that an upper limit of 4,000 IU (100 micrograms) per day is safe for adults and children over 11 years of age, including pregnant and lactating women (https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf). NHS prescribing policy, as stated by multiple ICBs, indicates that routine prescription of vitamin D supplements in primary care is not recommended unless specific exception criteria are met (https://www.nottsapc.nhs.uk/media/mmnbgqpv/vitamin-d-guidelines-adults.pdf).

