**Vitamin D — Prose Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, and that these nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth, and muscles healthy ([https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d)). The East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust additionally notes that vitamin D has a role in immune function and cell division, and has effects on many different organs and tissues ([https://www.esht.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/0926.pdf](https://www.esht.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/0926.pdf)).

According to NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly, most vitamin D comes from the action of sunlight on the skin; dietary sources include oily fish (such as salmon, mackerel, sardines, and herring), egg yolks, meat, milk, and fortified foods such as margarine and some breakfast cereals ([https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d](https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d)).

The NHS states that 10 micrograms (400 IU) a day is enough for most people ([https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d)), a figure consistent with NICE guidance, which specifies 10 micrograms per day for everyone in the general population aged 4 and older ([https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/chapter/glossary](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/chapter/glossary)). The NHS further states that adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly, should not exceed 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) per day ([https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d)).

