## Vitamin D — Evidence Summary

To address your question directly: the UK figure is not 600 IU. According to the NHS, the UK reference amount is **10 micrograms (400 IU) per day** for most people — notably lower than the US recommendation of 600 IU. NICE guidance, drawing on the Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN), similarly cites 10 micrograms per day for everyone in the general population aged 4 and older. I record and cite sources rather than advise on which figure to follow; the relevant sourced facts are set out below.

The NHS states that Vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, nutrients needed to keep bones, teeth, and muscles healthy ([NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d)). An East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust document additionally notes that Vitamin D has a role in immune function and cell division, and effects on many different organs and tissues ([ESHT](https://www.esht.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/0926.pdf)).

Food sources listed by NHS sources include oily fish (salmon, mackerel, trout, sardines, pilchards, herring, and canned tuna), egg yolks, meat, milk, liver, tofu, and mushrooms, as well as fortified foods such as margarine, some breakfast cereals, soya products, orange juice, and infant formula ([NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly ICB](https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d); [South West London ICB](https://swlimo.southwestlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/SWL-Vitamin-D-PIL-Accessible-V1.1.pdf)).

The NHS states the upper limit for adults is 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) per day ([NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d)).

