## Vitamin D — Evidence Summary

The NHS states that vitamin D "helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body" and that these nutrients "are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). East Sussex Healthcare NHS Trust additionally records that vitamin D "has a role in immune function and cell division" and "has effects on many different organs and tissues all over the body" (https://www.esht.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/0926.pdf).

Regarding food sources, NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly lists oily fish (salmon, mackerel, trout, sardines, pilchards, herring), canned tuna, egg yolks, meat, milk, margarine, and some breakfast cereals as sources of vitamin D (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d). South West London ICB also includes red meat, liver, tofu, and mushrooms, and notes that in the UK some margarine, soya products, breakfast cereals, orange juice, and infant formula are fortified with vitamin D (https://swlimo.southwestlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/SWL-Vitamin-D-PIL-Accessible-V1.1.pdf).

On the specific question asked — the UK recommended daily figure compared with the US figure of 600 IU — I record and cite sources rather than advise, but the relevant sourced facts are these: NHS Cornwall and Isles of Scilly, citing Public Health England, states that the UK recommendation is **10 micrograms (400 IU) per day** for everyone over the age of one, considered during autumn and winter months (https://cios.icb.nhs.uk/health/nutrition/vitamin-d). South West London ICB gives the same figure of 10 micrograms (400 units) (https://swlimo.southwestlondon.icb.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/SWL-Vitamin-D-Guidelines-for-Adults-and-Children.pdf). The UK figure of 400 IU is therefore lower than the US figure of 600 IU cited in your question, according to these UK NHS sources.

Regarding the upper limit, Buckinghamshire NHS Formulary records that both EFSA and the UK Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) concluded that **4,000 IU (100 micrograms) per day** is the safe upper limit for adults and children over 11 years (https://www.bucksformulary.nhs.uk/docs/Guideline_785FM.pdf), a figure consistent with SACN advice published via Sandwell and West Birmingham CCG Formulary (https://www.sandwellandwestbhamccgformulary.nhs.uk/docs/2016%20New%20PHE%20Vitamin%20D%20Advice%20FINAL.%20July%202016.pdf).

