The NHS states that iron "is important in making red blood cells, which carry oxygen around the body" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). Kingston and Richmond NHS Foundation Trust adds that iron "is needed to make haemoglobin… found in red blood cells," and that it "is also important for building and maintaining healthy muscles and for many of the body's functions and reactions" (https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet). Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust notes that iron "also plays a part in energy production and the immune system" (https://www.leedsth.nhs.uk/patients/resources/your-body-uses-iron-to-make-special-cells-that-carry-oxygen).

