**Folate — Prose Summary**

The NHS describes folate as a B vitamin found in many foods, noting that its manufactured form is called folic acid, and that it is also known as folacin and vitamin B9 (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). The NHS states that folate and vitamin B12 together perform several important functions in the body, including keeping the nervous system healthy (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia). According to the NHS, good dietary sources of folate include broccoli, Brussels sprouts, asparagus, peas, chickpeas, and brown rice (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia/causes). The NHS also notes that folate dissolves in water, meaning the body cannot store it for long periods — the body's store is usually sufficient to last around four months (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia/causes). The UK adult dietary reference value is 200 micrograms (0.2 mg) per day, as stated by both the Hull University Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust patient leaflet (https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/folate-information-for-renal-patients-information-to-increase-folate-in-your-diet) and the BDA Food Fact Sheet hosted by UH Sussex NHS Trust (https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf). The BDA Food Fact Sheet, hosted by UH Sussex NHS Trust, states that long-term intakes of folic acid from fortified foods and supplements should remain below 1 mg per day for adults, and that those aged over 50 or with a history of bowel cancer are advised not to take supplements containing more than 200 micrograms per day (https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf). Regarding regulatory status, the BNF lists folic acid as a drug with defined indications and doses (https://bnf.nice.org.uk/drugs/folic-acid); NHS Scotland's Right Decisions resource notes that the 5 mg dose is available only on prescription for women at increased risk of neural tube disorders (https://www.rightdecisions.scot.nhs.uk/dgrefhelp-nhs-dumfries-galloway/sexual-health/pregnancy-related/preconception-health/folic-acid-supplements), while the Northampton General Hospital NHS Formulary lists the 400 microgram tablet under "Self Care" for prevention of neural tube defects (https://www.northamptonformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09&SubSectionID=A100).

