## Folate — Evidence Summary

The NHS describes folate as one of the B vitamins and states that it "helps the body make healthy red blood cells," according to [NHS – B vitamins and folic acid](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). The Whittington NHS Trust states that folate "helps your body make DNA… It also helps make RNA… This means folate is important for making new cells," and notes it "is very important during pregnancy, infancy, adolescence, and any time you need to grow new tissue," per [Whittington NHS Trust – Folate (NLOSS)](https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=374). NHS 111 Wales states that folate, alongside vitamin B12, performs functions including "keeping the nervous system healthy," per [NHS 111 Wales – Anaemia, vitamin B12 and folate deficiency](https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/a/article/anaemia,vitaminb12andfolatedeficiency). The University Hospitals Sussex NHS Trust (citing the BDA) states that "folic acid, together with vitamin B12, is necessary to form red blood cells" and is "essential in the formation of DNA (genetic material) within every body cell," per [University Hospitals Sussex NHS Trust – Folic Acid fact sheet](https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

The NHS lists good dietary sources as "broccoli, brussels sprouts, leafy green vegetables, such as cabbage, kale, spring greens and spinach, peas, chickpeas and kidney beans, liver… breakfast cereals fortified with folic acid," per [NHS – B vitamins and folic acid](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). The Whittington NHS Trust notes that "the biggest source of folate in the British diet is in fortified foods such as cereals and bread," with natural sources including "oranges or orange juice, green vegetables, peanuts, peas, sprouts, liver and soybeans," per [Whittington NHS Trust – Folate (NLOSS)](https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=374).

The NHS states that adults need 200 micrograms of folate a day, per [NHS – B vitamins and folic acid](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b), a figure also given by Hull University Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust (expressed as 0.2 mg) per [Hull University Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust – Folate Information for Renal Patients](https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/folate-information-for-renal-patients-information-to-increase-folate-in-your-diet), and by the University Hospitals Sussex NHS Trust (citing the BDA) per [UH Sussex – Folic Acid fact sheet](https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

Regarding upper limits, the NHS states that "taking doses of folic acid higher than 1mg can mask the symptoms of vitamin B12 deficiency" and that "taking 1mg or less a day of folic acid is unlikely to cause any harm," per [NHS – B vitamins and folic acid](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). NHS 111 Wales states the same, per [NHS 111 Wales – Vitamins and minerals](http://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/v/article/Vitaminsandminerals).

On regulatory status, the Bedfordshire and Luton Joint Formulary classifies folic acid 400 microgram tablets as "Self Care" for "routine supplementation in pregnancy," while folic acid 5 mg must be prescribed, per [Bedfordshire and Luton Joint Formulary](https://www.bedsformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.01&SubSectionID=A100). An NHS Scotland Patient Group Direction document records folic acid 5 mg as a Prescription Only Medicine (POM), per [NHS Scotland – Folic Acid 5mg PGD](https://www.rightdecisions.scot.nhs.uk/media/1272/folic-acid-5-mg-pgd-v3.pdf). The Cambridgeshire and Peterborough Formulary states that "pregnant women with diabetes, epilepsy or a raised body mass index (BMI) will need a prescription for a higher dose of folic acid, 5mg per day," per [Cambridgeshire and Peterborough Formulary](https://www.cambridgeshireandpeterboroughformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09&SubSectionID=A100).

