**Folate — Evidence Summary**

According to NHS England, folate helps the body make healthy red blood cells, make DNA, and reduce the risk of certain birth defects called neural tube defects, such as spina bifida, during pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). The NHS also states that vitamin B12 and folate together perform several important functions, including keeping the nervous system healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia). The University Hospitals Sussex NHS Foundation Trust patient leaflet, sourced from the British Dietetic Association, states that folic acid is essential in the formation of DNA within every body cell, allowing each cell to replicate perfectly, and that together with vitamin B12 it is necessary to form red blood cells and helps nerves to function properly (https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

NHS England lists good food sources of folate as broccoli, Brussels sprouts, leafy green vegetables such as cabbage, kale, spring greens and spinach, peas, chickpeas and kidney beans, liver, and breakfast cereals fortified with folic acid (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). The Whittington Health NHS Trust additionally notes that the biggest source of folate in the British diet is fortified foods such as cereals and bread, and that natural sources include oranges or orange juice, green vegetables, peanuts, peas, sprouts, liver and soybeans (https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=374).

NHS 111 Wales states that adults need 200 micrograms of folate a day (http://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/v/article/Vitaminsandminerals), a figure consistent with that given by NHS England (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b) and confirmed by the UH Sussex NHS / BDA leaflet for adults and children over 11 years (https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

Regarding upper limits, NHS 111 Wales states that taking doses of folic acid higher than 1 mg can mask the symptoms of vitamin B12 deficiency (http://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/v/article/Vitaminsandminerals). The UH Sussex NHS / BDA leaflet states that long-term intakes of folic acid from fortified foods and supplements should be below 1 mg (1,000 micrograms) per day for most people, and that those aged over 50 or with a history of bowel cancer are advised not to take folic acid supplements containing more than 200 micrograms per day (https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

On regulatory status, the South West London NHS Joint Medicines Formulary, which links to BNF entries, records a 400-microgram strength of folic acid as a self-care (over-the-counter) medicine for routine use, while higher-dose forms are listed as prescription-only (https://www.swljointmedicinesformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09&SubSectionID=A100). The BSW NHS Formulary notes that high-dose folic acid 5 mg daily is to be prescribed for specific clinical indications, for example for women on anti-seizure medication who are pregnant or planning pregnancy (https://bswformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.01&SubSectionID=A100).

