## Zinc — Evidence Summary

According to the NHS, zinc contributes to making new cells and enzymes, processing carbohydrate, fat and protein in food, and wound healing. Sheffield Laboratory Medicine (NHS) additionally describes zinc as an essential trace element that forms a component of many enzymes and is involved in the modulation of gene transcription.

The NHS lists good dietary sources of zinc as meat, shellfish, dairy foods such as cheese, bread, and cereal products such as wheatgerm.

The NHS states that the reference intake is approximately 9.5 mg a day for men aged 19 to 64 and 7 mg a day for women. Sheffield Laboratory Medicine (NHS) cites a Reference Nutrient Intake of approximately 10 mg per day, without an age or sex breakdown.

Regarding the upper limit, the NHS states that no more than 25 mg of zinc supplements a day should be taken unless advised by a doctor, and that taking high doses of zinc reduces the amount of copper the body can absorb, which the NHS states can lead to anaemia and weakening of the bones.

On regulatory status, a NICE evidence review notes that zinc acetate dihydrate (brand name Wilzin) holds a UK marketing authorisation as a licensed medicine specifically for treating Wilson's disease, and that other zinc salts such as zinc sulfate and zinc gluconate have also been used in that context without being covered by the same licence. The Greater Manchester NHS Formulary lists zinc sulfate effervescent tablets as a formulary medicine under Chapter 9 (Nutrition and Blood), referencing the adult BNF.

